      "bhashyaExtract": "अत्र हेयं बन्धं निरूप्य तन्निवृत्तिनिबन्धनम् अखण्डार्थं ज्ञानमादेयत्वेनोपपाद्यते — पञ्चपादिका-तत्त्वप्रदीपिका-कल्पतरु-संमतम् अखण्डार्थत्वलक्षणं लक्षणामात्रसम्बन्धाद् ब्रह्मणि निर्दुष्टं साधितम् ।",
      "englishTranslation": "Opening the Second Pariccheda (Ātmanirūpaṇam — Exposition of the Self): Having examined bondage (the mithyā universe to be discarded) in Pariccheda 1, Madhusūdana now sets forth what is to be attained—the non-relational knowledge (akhaṇḍārtha-jñāna) that removes bondage. He classifies akhaṇḍārtha into word-centered (padārtha-niṣṭha, Vedic like 'Satyam Jñānam Anantam' and secular like 'Prakṛṣṭaprakāśaḥ [Candraḥ]') and sentence-centered (vākyārtha-niṣṭha, Vedic like 'Tat tvam asi' and secular like 'So'yaṁ Devadattaḥ').\n\nRefuting Vyāsatīrtha's attempts to undermine both definitions (Citsukha's 'non-synonymous words producing valid cognition untouched by any syntactic relation not already recalled by word-signification', and Padmapāda's 'culminating in a single bare stem-meaning without mutual qualification'), Madhusūdana specifies 'apart from relations recalled by word-vṛtti'—avoiding under-coverage in definitions like 'Saṁyoga is union of things previously unjoined' and over-coverage in ironic sentences like 'Eat poison!' (meaning 'Don't eat in an enemy's house'). Closing with Kalpataru's stanza, he explains how even attributeless Brahman can be indicated through apparent/superimposed relations, just as 'silver' indicates shell in illusion.",
      "commentaryNotes": "Inaugurates Pariccheda 2 by defending the classical Advaita semantics of akhaṇḍārthatva (non-relational linguistic import) against Nyāyāmṛta's Navya-Nyāya and Bhāṭṭa Mīmāṁsā objections.",
